---
// A featured band for Captive Origins, in its own palette (brown ink, cream, orange and pink
// from the public brand notes in the One tools). Everything here is already public: the live
// prototype site and the public One tools. No plans, prices or partners.
---

<section class="co" aria-labelledby="co-title">
  <div class="co-inner">
    <p class="co-eyebrow">My life's work</p>
    <h2 id="co-title">Captive Origins</h2>
    <p class="co-lede">
      A home for the records of animals in human care, starting with reptiles: what they are,
      where their families come from, and what the sources actually say.
    </p>

    <div class="co-cols">
      <div>
        <h3>Why it's needed</h3>
        <p>
          A lot of captive animals have no reliable record of who their parents were or where
          their line came from. What records there are sit in spreadsheets, on paper cards and in
          people's heads, and they're lost when an animal changes hands. Guesses get passed on as
          facts.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What it does</h3>
        <ul class="co-list">
          <li><strong>Taxonomy</strong> you can browse and search, in your own language.</li>
          <li><strong>Pedigrees</strong> that keep the unknown unknown, instead of filling gaps with guesses.</li>
          <li><strong>Lineage</strong> from genetics, with where the evidence came from and how sure it is.</li>
          <li><strong>Sources cited</strong>, and the people who use it vote on what gets built next.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Why me</h3>
        <p>
          It sits where my three interests meet. A pedigree is a family tree with different animals
          in it, and the same tools open both. Not many people are armchair genealogists, into
          reptiles and software engineers all at once. I happen to be all three.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="co-foot">
      <a class="co-link" href="https://captiveorigins.com">Visit captiveorigins.com</a>
      <span>Early days: the site is a prototype and accounts are invite-only for now. The
      frameworks underneath are source-available, under <a href="#science">Science tools</a>.</span>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Captive Origins palette. Text pairs meet WCAG AA; orange and pink are only used for decoration. */
